{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310650,"nonce":"VHVLEEY3L8CV4IYuxIzNVYnwmMc=","title":"Map\u003cK, V\u003e.get/set was returning garbage / failing JIT linking. Two compound bugs — @:multiType dispatch unimplemented + builtin mapping ...","message":"**Status: FIXED 2026-05-20.** `Map\u003cString, Int\u003e`, `Map\u003cInt, V\u003e`,\n`Map\u003cEnumValue, V\u003e`, `Map\u003cClass, V\u003e` all work end-to-end now. Haxe\nregression suite advanced 122→123/128 (`test_objectmap` started\npassing). See commit e26eab8.\n\n**Two compound fixes:**\n\n1. `tast_to_hir.rs::maybe_resolve_multitype_map` — intercepts\n   `new Map\u003cK, V\u003e(...)` at TAST New site, substitutes the concrete\n   underlying (StringMap / IntMap / EnumValueMap / ObjectMap) based\n   on K. Variable type then carries the concrete type forward;\n   method calls dispatch through the right stdlib mapping.\n\n2. `compilation.rs::collect_extern_method_entries` — the auto-\n   registration of extern class methods as `NativePlugin` mappings\n   was looking up the builtin override via the qualified class name\n   only (e.g. `haxe.ds.StringMap`), missing the builtin entries\n   registered with simple names (`StringMap`). The lookup now tries\n   underscore, dot, AND bare-name forms; the bare-name path stamps\n   in the correct `runtime_name` (`haxe_stringmap_set`).\n\nWithout (2), even after (1), `m.set(\"a\", 42)` failed at JIT link\ntime with \"can't resolve symbol set\" — the auto-registered bogus\nmapping `(class=\"haxe.ds.StringMap\", method=\"set\", runtime=\"set\")`\nwon the dispatch and emitted a CallDirect to a non-existent symbol.\n\n**The original analysis below is kept for reference.** It identified\nBalancedTree as the silent-fallthrough target, which was indirectly\ncorrect — fixing the @:multiType path eliminated the BalancedTree\ndetour, then fixing the builtin override eliminated the bogus\nmapping that wedged the dispatch even on the correct concrete type.\n\n---\n\n\n\nUser code:\n```haxe\nvar m = new Map\u003cString, Int\u003e();\nm.set(\"a\", 1);\nvar v = m.get(\"a\");   // v has HIR type Null\u003cInt\u003e\ntrace(v);              // silently produces nothing (no crash)\n```\n\nThe user's hypothesis — \"dyn box type ID is not properly threading\ndown\" — is correct in spirit but not at the FFI layer. The runtime\n`haxe_stringmap_get` extern is never called for `Map\u003cString, Int\u003e`.\n\n\n`Map\u003cString, V\u003e` is `@:multiType` in haxe-std and should dispatch to\n`haxe.ds.StringMap` (extern), which the stdlib mapping wires to\n`haxe_stringmap_get` (returns_raw_value: true). The compiler has a\npost-cast (`hir_to_mir.rs` ~line 14134) that converts the U64 back to\nthe concrete T after the call — recently extended to handle I32,\nI64, F64, F32, Bool, Ptr\u003cconcrete\u003e, with a fall-through to I64 for\ntype-erased pointers.\n\n\nTAST→HIR tracing (inserted `eprintln!` in\n`TypedExpressionKind::MethodCall`) shows the call resolves to a\nchain of internal BalancedTree methods — `setLoop`, `compare`,\n`balance`, `minBinding`, `removeMinBinding`, `merge`, etc — all on a\n`Class { symbol_id: SymbolId(314) }`. That symbol is the AVL\n`BalancedTree` from `haxe.ds.BalancedTree`. **Not StringMap, not\nthe runtime extern.**\n\nThe `@:multiType` dispatch is selecting the wrong target — probably\nfalling through to `BalancedTree` as the generic `IMap` fallback\ninstead of the type-specialised `StringMap` (or `IntMap` for Int\nkeys). That alone would still work *if* BalancedTree's pure-Haxe\nimplementation were correct; it isn't, because:\n\n1. BalancedTree stores values typed as the abstract's `V` type\n   parameter. When V is `Int`, the value should round-trip as i32.\n2. The tree compares keys via a polymorphic `compare(a, b)`. Comparing\n   `String` requires the runtime's string-compare path; the abstract\n   value boxing may strip the type tag needed for that dispatch.\n3. The Null\u003cT\u003e wrap on the return type of `get` requires the compiler\n   to box i32 back into a `DynamicValue*` carrying the i32 type tag,\n   so subsequent operations (null check, `trace`) can unwrap.\n\nThe trace silently fails because either the BalancedTree returns\na value whose dynamic type tag doesn't match what `trace` expects,\nor the null comparison path can't determine whether the tagged value\nis null. No SIGSEGV — the JIT runs to completion, but the relevant\nside effects don't fire.\n\n\nA. **Make @:multiType dispatch select StringMap for String keys.**\n   Inspect the abstract-resolution path in `tast/ast_lowering.rs`. If\n   `K = String`, replace the constructor target with `StringMap\u003cV\u003e`.\n   `Map.set` / `Map.get` then inline to `StringMap.set` / `.get`\n   which already route through the runtime extern + the typed\n   post-cast at `hir_to_mir.rs` ~14134.\n\nB. **Fix the BalancedTree value-type threading.** Trace the\n   value-storage path. The tree's value field is likely lowered to\n   `Dynamic` and the i32 value isn't being boxed with its type tag\n   on insert / unboxed on retrieve. The same boxing path that handles\n   `Null\u003cT\u003e` at the runtime call boundary needs to apply at the\n   user-Haxe class-field boundary for generic type parameter values.\n\nC. **Replace `BalancedTree` in haxe-std with a thin extern wrapper\n   over the runtime ObjectMap.** Avoids the pure-Haxe correctness\n   problem entirely; lets every Map\u003cK,V\u003e share the same fast\n   hash-table runtime. Largest blast radius (every Haxe program that\n   relies on `BalancedTree` semantics changes) but most beneficial\n   for perf — pure-Haxe AVL tree is asymptotically worse than a hash\n   table.\n\nRecommendation: **A** is the surgical fix and the lowest-risk path.\nOnce landed, the more general value-type-threading work (B) becomes\nnice-to-have rather than blocking.\n\n\nRayzor JIT-compiles to native machine code. Per-instruction cost is\n*identical* to equivalent Rust code; speed differences come from\nalgorithms (BalancedTree vs hash table), data layout (boxed Dynamic\nvs unboxed primitive), and runtime boundary overhead (Cranelift\ncall setup is heavier than Rust's). Aim for parity with Rust at\nevery layer; don't accept \"pure Haxe is slow\" as a permanent\ntrade-off.\n\nThe `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.push` + read-back bug (separate from this one)\nfits the same pattern: pure-Haxe class with Dynamic-typed value\nstorage where the type tag isn't preserved through the boxed\nrepresentation.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_map_string_v_get` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}